# Host settings of rdtsc_search.sh and alkhaser_search.sh. Copy this file to site.conf (not
# tracked) next to it and fill it in; SITE_CONF=<file> picks another one. README.md, "Adapting it
# to your hardware", explains each value.
#
# Keep the VAR=${VAR:-value} form: a value given on the command line then still wins over the
# one here (DOM0=root@other ./rdtsc_search.sh). Settings of one run (TESTS, ONLY, VARIANTS,
# DRAKVUF_TIMEOUT, ...) belong on the command line, not here.

# --- dom0 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How ssh reaches dom0 as root, with a key that needs no password (the search runs for hours)
DOM0=${DOM0:-root@192.0.2.10}
SSH_KEY=${SSH_KEY:-$HOME/.ssh/id_ed25519}
# dom0's checkout of this repository, with make xen and make sandboxd run from it. It must be at
# the same commit as this checkout: the search compares their drakvuf submodules and Xen patch
REMOTE_REPO=${REMOTE_REPO:-/root/drakvuf-multios-sandbox}
# Where the search keeps its state on dom0 (installed variant, job logs, detector, al-khaser)
REMOTE_STATE=${REMOTE_STATE:-/root/evasion-tests}
# Where the benchmarks write on dom0. An al-khaser test writes tens of GB: a disk with room
REMOTE_OUT_BASE=${REMOTE_OUT_BASE:-/root/evasion-tests/out}

# --- Test VMs (README.md, "Test VMs") --------------------------------------------------------
# Each VM: the Xen cfg on dom0 the benchmark restores the saved state with, and the thin
# snapshot it restores it onto, which the cfg's disk must name. VM2 runs the second
# configuration at the same time as VM1; leave it empty when dom0 has no room for two guests.
LINUX_VM1=${LINUX_VM1-CFG=/root/evasion-tests/ubuntu_test1.cfg CLONE_NAME=ubuntu_sandbox-test1}
LINUX_VM2=${LINUX_VM2-CFG=/root/evasion-tests/ubuntu_test2.cfg CLONE_NAME=ubuntu_sandbox-test2}
WINDOWS_VM1=${WINDOWS_VM1-CFG=/root/evasion-tests/windows_test1.cfg CLONE_NAME=windows_sandbox-test1}
WINDOWS_VM2=${WINDOWS_VM2-CFG=/root/evasion-tests/windows_test2.cfg CLONE_NAME=windows_sandbox-test2}

# --- Hardware --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CPUs the Xen build is pinned to (a taskset list), empty for all. For a CPU that crashes the
# toolchain under a full build, like the i9-13900KF this was written on (its E-cores: 16-31)
BUILD_CPUS=${BUILD_CPUS-}
# Seconds dom0 may take to reboot, POST included
BOOT_LIMIT=${BOOT_LIMIT:-1800}

# --- Optional --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1 opens a VNC viewer per VM here while the tests run (TigerVNC). The cfgs from misc/xen-vms
# listen on 127.0.0.1: forward the ports with ssh -L and set VNC_HOST=127.0.0.1
VNC=${VNC:-0}
#VNC_HOST=${VNC_HOST:-127.0.0.1}
#VNC_PORTS=${VNC_PORTS:-5900 5901}
# Where the results go here: <RESULTS_BASE>/rdtsc-search/<timestamp>, .../alkhaser-search/...
#RESULTS_BASE=${RESULTS_BASE:-/path/to/results}
# A Xen tree of your own instead of dom0's make xen tree, e.g. while developing the patch: every
# file it changed from XEN_BASE is uploaded to REMOTE_XEN. Its hvmloader and libacpi lack the
# guest identity (scripts/xen-identity.py patch-xen): run make xen again after such a search.
#LOCAL_XEN=${LOCAL_XEN:-$HOME/src/xen}
#XEN_BASE=${XEN_BASE:-RELEASE-4.20.1}
#REMOTE_XEN=${REMOTE_XEN:-/root/src/xen}
# 0 skips the check that dom0's drakvuf source is this checkout's (it is still built there)
#DRAKVUF_SOURCE_CHECK=${DRAKVUF_SOURCE_CHECK:-1}
# Added to every benchmark's environment, last: e.g. another DRAKVUF build
#BENCH_EXTRA_ENV=${BENCH_EXTRA_ENV-DEV_BIN=/opt/drakvuf/build/drakvuf DEV_LIBVMI=/opt/libvmi/libvmi/.libs}
# The detector on dom0 and in the guest (names ending in /detector); BUILD_DETECTOR=0 uses an
# existing binary instead of building detector.c on dom0
#REMOTE_DETECTOR=${REMOTE_DETECTOR:-/root/evasion-tests/detector}
#DETECTOR_DST=${DETECTOR_DST:-/tmp/detector}
#BUILD_DETECTOR=${BUILD_DETECTOR:-1}